# Erc7984

_Reference for zama-solidity skill (zama-protocol plugin)_

# ERC-7984 Integration

Use the installed [OpenZeppelin ERC-7984 implementation](https://github.com/OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-confidential-contracts/blob/master/contracts/token/ERC7984/ERC7984.sol) and its [interfaces/extensions](https://github.com/OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-confidential-contracts/tree/master/contracts/token/ERC7984). Check compatible package versions before choosing an extension; do not reproduce a token implementation or a full API table here.

- **Follow the value.** A financial contract needs actual transfers, custody and refunds, or must clearly expose that it only records assertions. Custom encrypted balances/allowances duplicate ERC-7984's security responsibilities.
- **Import external inputs at their target.** An auction importing a proof bound to itself forwards the resulting `euint64` to the token overload with `FHE.allowTransient`. Passing that original proof to the token's external-input overload uses the wrong target. The holder must separately authorise the auction as an operator.
- **Use the returned transferred amount.** Transfers can silently return encrypted zero. Preserve payment checks before crediting bids or shares; receiver hooks also affect transfer acceptance.
- **Separate permissions.** Token operators authorise transfers; decryption delegation authorises reading. Inspect any ACL grants the transfer creates rather than treating the two permission systems as equivalent.
- **Use actual units.** The base token defaults to six decimals; extensions can differ. Wrappers expose `decimals()` and `rate()` independently of their underlying ERC-20. Check ranges before narrowing and avoid floating-point accounting.
- **Resolve overloads explicitly** when ethers cannot distinguish external-input/proof calls from existing-handle calls.

For wrapper discovery, load **zama-protocol** → `references/addresses.md`. For custody/payment examples, read `code-map.md`; use Etherscan's verified sources instead of private app repositories.
